WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.475
Performance criterion 4.2: check the security solution's scan logs for failed actions.

2
00:00:06.475 --> 00:00:10.148
A failed action means the threat may still be there.

3
00:00:10.148 --> 00:00:16.648
In this demonstration I search the school's scan log for the five failure events in the SOP.

4
00:00:17.747 --> 00:00:19.884
Rule F2 lists them.

5
00:00:19.884 --> 00:00:24.158
1118 and 1119: the remediation failed.

6
00:00:24.158 --> 00:00:28.657
1002: a scan stopped before it finished.

7
00:00:28.657 --> 00:00:31.131
1005: the scan failed.

8
00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:33.830
2001: the update failed.

9
00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:42.827
Everything else, like 1001 scan finished or 1117 action taken, is not a failure.

10
00:00:43.927 --> 00:00:49.971
grep dash E with the five IDs between commas finds only those event IDs.

11
00:00:49.971 --> 00:00:56.855
Two failures: REG-PC-01 could not quarantine a trojan because the file was in use,

12
00:00:56.855 --> 00:01:01.975
and on MKT-PC-04 the scan itself failed with an engine error.

13
00:01:03.075 --> 00:01:11.042
To be sure nothing is hidden, Group-Object counts every event ID in the log.

14
00:01:11.042 --> 00:01:14.711
Four lines: 1001, 1118, 1005, 2000.

15
00:01:14.711 --> 00:01:17.331
Two of them are failures.

16
00:01:18.431 --> 00:01:20.894
Two failed actions to escalate.

17
00:01:20.894 --> 00:01:25.103
Which person gets each one is the next demonstration.

18
00:01:26.203 --> 00:01:27.113
Your turn.

19
00:01:27.113 --> 00:01:32.022
In the CTM Virtual Lab, choose Day 9 and Activity 9.2.

20
00:01:32.022 --> 00:01:35.749
Thirteen lines from the bank's scan logs.

21
00:01:35.749 --> 00:01:38.022
Find every failed action.

22
00:01:38.022 --> 00:01:42.932
Watch out for events that look scary but mean success.

